// Brought to u by MidManStudio
//
// AS3a: Task.spawn and join on real worker threads. This template edits existing docs and site pages.
// The code, the tests, the fixtures and the README come in the tarball as3a-spawn-replacements.tar.gz.
// Anchored edits only (each anchor occurs exactly once and none is a whole line):
//   docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md                    AS3a decisions, section 1, items 1, 3, 6 and 7 of section 5, section 3 cost, the AS3 rows
//   docs/PARSER_RULES.md                         a new section 5.7d on Task before a dot
//   docs/PARKED_IDEAS.md                         a section of AS3a findings
//   web/site/src/guide/async.md                  a new section on running tasks on other threads, and the status sentence
//   web/site/src/project-status.md               what landed
//   docs/MODULE_DESIGN.md                        std.task in the table behind summon std
//   crates/core/src/sema/type_infer.rs           Task.spawn and join typing: three insertions (the file is shared with another chat, so no whole-file copy)
// This overwrites the earlier patch.mdix, which is right: there is only ever one.
// Checked on a fresh clone of live with the real applier.

@CONFIG(
  version    -> "1.0.0",
  author     -> "MidManStudio",
  features   -> "quickfuncs,data",
  debug_mode -> "off"
)

@QUICKFUNCS(
  ~patch_replace_text<object>(_path, _anchor, _content) {
    return { path = _path, op = "replace_text", anchor = _anchor, content = _content }
  }
)

@DATA(
  patch_files::
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "## 1. Where things stand (probed 2026-11-14 on live master", "### AS3a decisions (2026-11-19)\n\n- Decided with the user: the interpreter keeps `Rc` inside each task, with a deep copy at the task\n  boundary and a thread-safe backing for the shared payloads of later slices (item 1 of section 5,\n  as recommended). The default pool has one worker fewer than the logical cores, at least one, with\n  no pin or affinity settings, and an engine that pins threads brings its own executor (item 3, as\n  recommended). `Task.spawn(t)` returns the same `Task<T>`, now running, which is awaited or joined\n  with `join()` from synchronous code, and `join()` on a task that was never spawned runs it in\n  place (item 7, as recommended). AS3 is sliced in two: AS3a is built, AS3b is the executor\n  interface, a replaceable pool, the park hook and the worker-count setting. `Send` and `Sync` are\n  explicit opt-in impls (item 6, **against the recommendation**, built in AS4, cost in section 3).\n- Settled without a question:\n  - A program that mentions `Task.spawn` runs inside a thread scope that holds the workers, and each\n    worker owns an interpreter built from the same program, so a declared function has the same id\n    in all of them. A program that never mentions `Task.spawn` starts no thread. Where there are no\n    threads (`wasm32`, or a worker count of zero) `Task.spawn` is a run-time error that says so. A\n    worker has a 128 MiB stack.\n  - The copy at the boundary moved from AS4 to AS3a, because nothing can be passed to a task\n    without it. It keeps what was shared inside one copy shared and rebuilds a cycle as a cycle. A\n    value that is not plain data (`Shared`, `SyncShared`, a task, a `Pool`, a handle, a query, a\n    closure) cannot be sent and is a run-time error that names its kind, until AS4 makes it a\n    compile-time one. A function declared in the program can be sent, a lambda cannot.\n  - An `async { }` block carries the names it mentions that are bound in the code around it, found\n    by name, which can be more than it uses. Constants and functions of the program are not\n    copied, because every worker has them.\n  - A task is awaited at most once, and `await` and `join` are the same thing for it. A worker that\n    awaits a task that no worker has taken yet runs it itself, so a worker never waits for a thread\n    that waits for it. The thread that started the pool only waits, so no task runs against the\n    statics of the program.\n  - A task that fails hands its failure value to the one that awaits it, and a panic arrives with\n    its message. A task whose result cannot be sent panics with a message that says so.\n  - A task sees the constants and statics as they were when the pool started. A write to a static\n    from a task is a run-time error. The contents of a collection held in a static are a private\n    copy in each worker, so a change to them is seen only by that worker, by later tasks on it too.\n    A constant whose value cannot be sent (a closure) is not there in a task.\n  - What a worker prints goes to the same output as the rest of the run, also when the output is\n    captured.\n  - `Task` is a builtin namespace, so the table behind `summon std.*` has to place it: it is the only\n    member of a new `std.task` module. That grouping is a first cut (`docs/MODULE_DESIGN.md`, open\n    questions), so it can move while no program depends on it. The locks and channels of AS5 are\n    expected in `std.sync`.\n- Known gaps, recorded in `docs/PARKED_IDEAS.md`: the entries a task makes in the function table\n  are removed only when nothing was added after them, a scope or lock that outlives one `await` is\n  not there yet (AS5, AS6), and the native backend has no threads.\n\n## 1. Where things stand (probed 2026-11-14 on live master")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "`using` when AS1 landed, 2026-11-18)", "`using` when AS1 landed, 2026-11-18, threads when AS3a landed, 2026-11-19)")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "No `Mutex`, `Channel`, `spawn`, `Thread` or `Atomic` exists (`NAME-001` for each).", "`Task.spawn` and `join` exist (AS3a, 2026-11-19). No `Mutex`, `Channel`, `Thread` or `Atomic` does (`NAME-001` for each).")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "so `Value` is not `Send`.", "so `Value` is not `Send`. A value crosses to another\n  thread as a `Sent` (`interpreter/send.rs`), plain data with no `Rc`.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "`statics`, and `pool_capacity_stack`.", "`statics`, and `pool_capacity_stack`. Each worker thread owns an interpreter of its own\n  (`interpreter/eval/spawn.rs`).")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "- The only global state is the thread-local `OUTPUT_CAPTURE` (output capture for tests and the\n  playground) and an atomic logger flag. A spawned thread would not see the capture.", "- The only global state is the thread-local `OUTPUT_CAPTURE` (output capture for tests and the\n  playground) and an atomic logger flag. A run with workers moves the capture into a buffer that\n  the workers share, so what a worker prints is captured too.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "(a) stays the fallback because it is mechanical and cheap.", "(a) stays the fallback because it is mechanical and cheap.\n   *Decided 2026-11-19:* (b), as recommended. Built in AS3a.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "thread names and affinity for engines that pin\n   threads.", "thread names and affinity for engines that pin\n   threads. *Decided 2026-11-19:* logical cores minus one, at least one, and no pin or affinity\n   settings on the default pool. An engine that pins threads brings its own executor. The\n   interface itself is AS3b.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "derived structurally, writable as bounds.", "derived structurally, writable as bounds.\n   *Decided 2026-11-19, against the recommendation:* `Send` and `Sync` are explicit opt-in impls, so\n   nothing is sendable until a type says so. Built in AS4. Cost: section 3. Still open for AS4:\n   whether the compiler checks an impl against the fields or trusts it.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "`Task.spawn` needs the parser to accept `Task` followed by `.` in expression\n   position.", "`Task.spawn` needs the parser to accept `Task` followed by `.` in expression\n   position. *`Task.spawn` and `join` decided 2026-11-19 and built in AS3a:* `Task.spawn(t)` returns\n   the same `Task<T>`, running, `join()` waits for it, and the parser accepts `Task` before a `.`.\n   `Task.scope`, `Task.cancelled`, the lock type and the ordering names stay open.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "| **AS3** | Threads in the interpreter: the state split, the pool, `Task.spawn`, join, the executor interface, the park hook. |", "| **AS3a** | **Done 2026-11-19.** Threads in the interpreter: a worker interpreter on each thread, the copy at the task boundary (moved here from AS4), the pool, `Task.spawn`, `join`. |")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "| **AS4** | The sendable rule: marker traits, the sema pass, copy at the boundary, a real `SyncShared`, `TIER-014` lifted for it. |", "| **AS3b** | The executor interface, a replaceable pool, the park hook, the worker-count setting. | AS3a |\n| **AS4** | The sendable rule: explicit `Send` and `Sync` impls, the sema pass, a real `SyncShared`, `TIER-014` lifted for it. |")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "themselves, so a lock guard is not exposed to it. If the trap shows up in practice, a warning for a\n`using` binding whose type has a method named `dispose` but no `Dispose` impl is cheap to add.", "themselves, so a lock guard is not exposed to it. If the trap shows up in practice, a warning for a\n`using` binding whose type has a method named `dispose` but no `Dispose` impl is cheap to add.\n\n**AS4, `Send` and `Sync` are explicit opt-in impls.** The recommendation was marker traits derived\nstructurally from the fields. As decided, a struct or enum that crosses into a task needs an\n`impl Send` of its own, and forgetting it is a compile error, so the table of section 2.3 (which says\n\"of sendable parts\") is rewritten at AS4. The built-in types (`List`, `Dictionary`, tuples, arrays,\nclosures) still need compiler rules that depend on their parts, because conditional impls on built-in\ntypes are not built (`TYPE-129`). It is open whether an `impl Send` is checked against the fields or\ntrusted. A checked one adds only the opt-in. A trusted one lets a struct that holds a `Shared<T>` be\ndeclared sendable, which is a data race the compiler cannot see. A generic library can still write\n`T: Send` as a bound.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/PARSER_RULES.md", "(`TYPE-144`) are type inference's rules.", "(`TYPE-144`) are type inference's rules.\n\n### 5.7d `Task` before a `.`\n\n`Task` is a reserved word, for the benefit of type syntax (`Task<T>`). In expression position it is\nalso the name of a static object, as `List` is in `List.new()`, so that `Task.spawn(t)` parses. The\nparser takes `Task` as a name only when the next token is a `.`: `parse_prefix` routes it through\n`parse_ident_expr` as it does the collection keywords, and `Parser::can_start_expr` answers the same\nway, so that `return Task.spawn(t).join()` reads a value (`tests/call_and_return_parsing.rs`). A\n`Task` anywhere else in an expression is still an error. What `Task.spawn` and `join` mean is type\ninference's rule and the interpreter's; the parser reads an ordinary call.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/PARKED_IDEAS.md", "- A `let mut` binding that is assigned inside the block is cleaned up with the value it started\n  with. The replacement is not cleaned up.", "- A `let mut` binding that is assigned inside the block is cleaned up with the value it started\n  with. The replacement is not cleaned up.\n\n## Found while building AS3a (verified by execution, parked)\n\n- A static that holds a collection is a private copy in each worker. A task that changes its\n  contents (`LOG.push(..)`) changes only the copy of the worker it runs on, and a later task on the\n  same worker sees the change. Assigning to the static is refused; changing what it holds is not\n  noticed. A shared value (AS4) is the fix.\n- A `const` whose value is a closure is not copied to the workers, so a task that uses it fails with\n  an undefined name. A function declared in the program is fine.\n- An `async { }` block carries every name it mentions that is bound around it, found by name\n  without scoping, so a name the block declares itself can pull in an outer value of the same name\n  that is then not used. If that value cannot be sent (a `Shared`), the spawn fails for a value the\n  block never touches.\n- The function-table entry made for an `async { }` block that runs on a worker is removed when the\n  block ends, but only if no function was added after it. A block that creates a closure keeps its\n  entry, so a worker that runs many such blocks grows its table.\n- `Task.spawn` is refused in a run with no workers. The browser playground has none.")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/guide/async.md", "The decided design (the async core is built, threads are not)", "Running tasks on other threads\n\n`Task.spawn(task)` hands a task to a pool of worker threads and gives the same task back, now\nrunning. Wait for it with `await` in async code, or with `.join()` anywhere. Both give the result\nof the task, and a task is awaited or joined once.\n\n<!-- ubel-test: run -->\n```ubel\nasync fn double(n: int) Task<int> {\n    return n * 2\n}\n\nfn main() void {\n    let a = Task.spawn(double(1))\n    let b = Task.spawn(double(2))\n    println(a.join() + b.join())\n}\n```\n\n```text\n6\n```\n\nA program that never writes `Task.spawn` starts no threads, and `.join()` on a task that was never\nspawned runs it right there. An `async { }` block can be spawned too, and it takes the names it\nuses from the code around it.\n\nWhat goes into a task is **copied**. A change made inside a task is not seen by the code that\nspawned it, what was shared inside the copy stays shared, and the result comes back as a copy. A\nvalue that cannot be copied is refused, when the program runs, with an error that names it: a\n`Shared`, a `SyncShared`, a task, a `Pool` or a closure. A function declared in the program can be\nsent. A task sees the constants and statics as they were when the pool started, and it cannot\nwrite a static.\n\nA panic in a task reaches the one that awaits it, with its message, and a `fail` arrives as a\nfailure. The pool has one worker fewer than the machine has logical cores, and at least one. There\nare no threads in the browser playground, so `Task.spawn` reports an error there.\n\n## The decided design (the async core and real threads are built, sharing is not)")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/guide/async.md", "The async core and the `using` cleanup are built (see\n[Generics and traits](./generics-traits.md#cleanup-with-using-and-dispose)). Delivery continues with\nthreads in the interpreter and the sharing rule.", "The async core, the `using` cleanup and tasks on real threads are built (see\n[Generics and traits](./generics-traits.md#cleanup-with-using-and-dispose) for `using`). Delivery\ncontinues with the sharing rule, the lock types and the executor an engine can replace.")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/project-status.md", "Next: threads in the interpreter", "Tasks run on real threads too\n  (2026-11-19): `Task.spawn` hands a task to a pool of workers, values are copied at the boundary, and\n  `join()` waits. Next: the sharing rule and the lock types")
    patch_replace_text("docs/MODULE_DESIGN.md", "(`Pool`, `Unique`, `Shared`, `SyncShared`), `std.io`", "(`Pool`, `Unique`, `Shared`, `SyncShared`), `std.task` (`Task`), `std.io`")
    patch_replace_text("crates/core/src/sema/type_infer.rs", "                // `Trait.method(value, ..)`: the explicit trait-qualified call.\n                if let ExprKind::Field { target, field } = callee.kind {\n", "                // AS3a, docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md: `Task.spawn(task)` hands a task to the worker\n                // threads and gives the same task back, running. It is valid in every tier.\n                if let ExprKind::Field { target, field } = callee.kind {\n                    if field == \"spawn\" {\n                        if let ExprKind::Ident(ns) = target.kind {\n                            if self.builtin_ns_name(target.span, ns) == \"Task\" {\n                                return self.infer_task_spawn(args, expr.span);\n                            }\n                        }\n                    }\n                }\n\n                // `Trait.method(value, ..)`: the explicit trait-qualified call.\n                if let ExprKind::Field { target, field } = callee.kind {\n")
    patch_replace_text("crates/core/src/sema/type_infer.rs", "                // §8 FIX (MEMORY_MODEL.md) — builtin instance methods\n                // (`myList.push(x)`, `\"s\".to_upper()`, ...). The generic\n", "                // AS3a: `task.join()` awaits a task from code that is not async, and gives its\n                // result. Valid in every tier, and the only method a task has.\n                if let ExprKind::Field { target, field } = callee.kind {\n                    if field == \"join\" {\n                        let receiver_ty = self.ctx.expr_type(target.span)\n                            .map(|ty| { let ty = self.default_if_int_lit(ty); self.apply(ty) });\n                        let inner = receiver_ty.and_then(|rt| match self.ctx.types.get(rt) {\n                            SemaType::Task(inner) => Some(*inner),\n                            _ => None,\n                        });\n                        if let Some(inner) = inner {\n                            if !args.is_empty() {\n                                self.errors.add_type_error(TypeError::ArgumentCountMismatch {\n                                    expected: 0,\n                                    found:    args.len(),\n                                    span:     expr.span,\n                                });\n                            }\n                            return inner;\n                        }\n                    }\n                }\n\n                // §8 FIX (MEMORY_MODEL.md) — builtin instance methods\n                // (`myList.push(x)`, `\"s\".to_upper()`, ...). The generic\n")
    patch_replace_text("crates/core/src/sema/type_infer.rs", "    fn unwrap_task(&mut self, ty: TypeId, span: Span) -> TypeId {\n        let ty = self.default_if_int_lit(ty);\n", "    /// `Task.spawn(task)`: the type of the call is the type of the task it is given.\n    fn infer_task_spawn<'ast>(&mut self, args: &'ast [Arg<'ast>], span: Span) -> TypeId {\n        if args.len() != 1 {\n            self.errors.add_type_error(TypeError::ArgumentCountMismatch {\n                expected: 1,\n                found:    args.len(),\n                span,\n            });\n            return self.unknown();\n        }\n        let arg_ty = match &args[0].kind {\n            ArgKind::Positional(e)       => self.infer_expr(e),\n            ArgKind::Named { value, .. } => self.infer_expr(value),\n        };\n        let arg_ty = self.default_if_int_lit(arg_ty);\n        let resolved = self.apply(arg_ty);\n        match self.ctx.types.get(resolved) {\n            SemaType::Task(_) => resolved,\n            SemaType::Var(_) => {\n                let inner = self.fresh_var();\n                let task  = self.ctx.types.intern(SemaType::Task(inner));\n                self.unify(resolved, task, span);\n                task\n            }\n            _ => {\n                self.errors.add_type_error(TypeError::TypeMismatch {\n                    expected:   \"Task<T>\".to_string(),\n                    found:      self.display_type(arg_ty),\n                    span,\n                    because_of: None,\n                });\n                TypeId::ERROR\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn unwrap_task(&mut self, ty: TypeId, span: Span) -> TypeId {\n        let ty = self.default_if_int_lit(ty);\n")
)
